body.proof_tex:
Primitivity and coprimality fix the denominator of the upstream identifying functional up to a nonzero common scalar; the argument never multiplies a new flag factor into both numerator and denominator.  Under the three algebraic assumptions, the complete classification gives
\[
 D\text{ is exactly self-normalizing}
 \quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
 D=\lambda_0\Delta_1^a\Delta_2^b\delta^e
\]
for one nested flag and nonnegative integers with \(a+b+e>0\).  For rational coefficients, the rational-recognition theorem decides this condition from the primitive reduced input and returns the stated exact certificate.

Now impose Gaussian sampling and acceptance.  The cited flag-power pivot gives, with \(m=(a+b+e,b+e,e)\),
\[
 \frac{D(\widehat\Sigma)}{D(\Sigma)}
 \stackrel{d}{=}
 W_m=
 \prod_{j=1}^{3}
 \left(\frac{\chi^2_{n-j+1}}{n}\right)^{m_j}, \tag{15}
\]
where the chi-squared variables are independent.  Positive definiteness of \(\Sigma\), full row rank of the flag bases, and the flag form imply \(D(\Sigma)\neq0\).  Also \(m_1=a+b+e>0\).  Thus \(W_m\) is positive and has a continuous distribution, so its lower and upper quantiles have exact tail probabilities \(\eta/2\).  Substituting \(d_0=D(\Sigma)\) in the definition of \(\mathcal I_D(\eta)\) and using (15) yields
\[
 \begin{aligned}
 \Pr_{\Sigma}\{D(\Sigma)\in\mathcal I_D(\eta)\}
 &=\Pr_{\Sigma}\!\left\{
 q_m(\eta/2)
 \leq \frac{D(\widehat\Sigma)}{D(\Sigma)}
 \leq q_m(1-\eta/2)
 \right\}\\
 &=\Pr\{q_m(\eta/2)\leq W_m\leq q_m(1-\eta/2)\}
 =1-\eta.
 \end{aligned}
\]
The conclusion concerns the identified denominator value \(D(\Sigma)\).  No confidence statement for the distinct causal ratio \(\tau(\Sigma)=N(\Sigma)/D(\Sigma)\) is used or implied.
